  ><TabPanel id="wan-internet"
    ><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="wifi" /> Broadband</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> The same type of internet service you have at home &mdash; cable,
        DSL, or fiber delivered by an ISP. Your traffic <strong>shares bandwidth</strong> with other customers
        in your area.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>
          <strong>Asymmetrical speeds:</strong> Download faster than upload (e.g., 500 Mbps down / 50
          Mbps up)
        </li>
        <li><strong>Shared bandwidth:</strong> Speed can vary based on neighborhood congestion</li>
        <li><strong>No SLA:</strong> Provider doesn&rsquo;t guarantee uptime or speeds</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Best use:</strong> Low-priority backup link, small remote offices, guest Wi-Fi
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tip">
        <Icon name="lightbulb" />
        <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Broadband makes a great <strong>backup link</strong> for SD-WAN &mdash;
        keep it cheap and let SD-WAN fail over to it only when the primary DIA is down.
      </div>
    </div><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="mobile-screen" /> FIA &mdash; Fixed Internet Access</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> A step up from basic broadband &mdash; a <strong
          >business-grade internet service</strong
        > with better reliability and basic support, but still <em>shared</em> bandwidth in the provider
        network.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>
          <strong>Business SLA:</strong> Basic uptime commitment (e.g., 99.9%), faster repair times than
          residential
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Higher speeds:</strong> 50 Mbps to 10 Gbps depending on technology and location
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Shared last mile:</strong> Still contended in the provider network, just with fewer
          users
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Middle ground:</strong> More reliable than broadband but less expensive than DIA
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="house" /> DIA &mdash; Dedicated Internet Access</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> A <strong>private internet connection just for you</strong>.
        Unlike broadband where you share a pipe with neighbors, DIA gives you <strong
          >guaranteed, symmetrical bandwidth</strong
        > that nobody else touches.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li><strong>Symmetrical speeds:</strong> Upload = Download (e.g., 500 Mbps both ways)</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guaranteed bandwidth:</strong> If you pay for 100 Mbps, you get 100 Mbps 24/7
        </li>
        <li><strong>SLA included:</strong> Uptime guarantees (99.99%), repair time commitments</li>
        <li><strong>Delivery:</strong> Over dedicated fiber, Ethernet, or T1/T3 (legacy)</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tip">
        <Icon name="lightbulb" />
        <strong>Analogy:</strong> Broadband = flying commercial (shared, delays possible). DIA = your
        own private runway (always available, guaranteed capacity).
      </div>
    </div></TabPanel
  ><TabPanel id="wan-private"
    ><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="tag" /> MPLS &mdash; Multiprotocol Label Switching</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> A <strong>private carrier network</strong> that connects your sites
        together. Your traffic never touches the public internet &mdash; it travels on the provider&rsquo;s
        private backbone using &ldquo;labels&rdquo; to route packets efficiently.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>
          <strong>How it works:</strong> The provider puts a &ldquo;label&rdquo; on your packets at the
          edge and switches them through their network based on that label (faster than reading full IP
          headers)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>QoS (Quality of Service):</strong> Can prioritize voice/video over data &mdash; critical
          for real-time applications
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Any-to-any:</strong> All sites can talk to each other directly (hub-and-spoke or full
          mesh)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Private:</strong> Your traffic is isolated from other customers (like a VPN but at the
          provider level)
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="result-grid mt-[10px]">
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Pros</div><div class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2">
            Guaranteed QoS, low latency, high reliability, SLA-backed
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Cons</div><div class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2">
            Expensive, long lead times, inflexible bandwidth changes, vendor lock-in
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Being Replaced By</div><div
            class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
          >
            SD-WAN (for many use cases, not all)
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="warn mt-[10px]">
        <Icon name="triangle-exclamation" />️ <strong>MPLS is NOT internet:</strong> MPLS connects your
        sites privately but does NOT give you internet access. You still need a separate DIA or broadband
        circuit for internet.
      </div>
    </div><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="building" /> Metro Ethernet (MetroE)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> High-speed <strong
          >Ethernet-based connectivity within a metro area</strong
        >, delivered over carrier fiber. It extends your LAN across the city as if both locations
        were plugged into the same switch.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>
          <strong>Layer 2 service:</strong> Operates at the data link layer &mdash; transparent to your
          routers
        </li>
        <li><strong>Service types (MEF standard):</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="table-wrap mt-[8px]">
        <table class="ref-table">
          <thead
            ><tr><th>MEF Service</th><th>Name</th><th>Topology</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead
          >
          <tbody>
            <tr
              ><td><strong>E-Line</strong></td><td>EPL / EVPL</td><td>Point-to-Point</td><td
                >Dedicated or virtual private line between 2 sites</td
              ></tr
            >
            <tr
              ><td><strong>E-LAN</strong></td><td>EP-LAN / EVP-LAN</td><td
                >Multipoint-to-Multipoint</td
              ><td>All sites talk directly to all other sites (like a big switch)</td></tr
            >
            <tr
              ><td><strong>E-Tree</strong></td><td>EP-Tree / EVP-Tree</td><td>Hub-and-Spoke</td><td
                >Branches talk to HQ but not to each other</td
              ></tr
            >
            <tr
              ><td><strong>E-Access</strong></td><td>Access EPL/EVPL</td><td>Inter-carrier</td><td
                >Extends service across provider boundaries</td
              ></tr
            >
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="tip mt-[10px]">
        <Icon name="lightbulb" />
        <strong>EPL vs EVPL:</strong> EPL = one customer, one circuit, full dedicated port. EVPL = multiple
        virtual circuits can share one physical port using VLAN tags. EPL costs more but gives dedicated
        bandwidth.
      </div>
    </div><div class="panel">
      <h3>Dark Fiber</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> You lease <strong>raw, unlit fiber optic strands</strong> from a
        provider. The fiber has no electronics on it &mdash; you supply your own optics (SFPs/QSFPs) and
        equipment on each end. &ldquo;Dark&rdquo; means the fiber has no light/signal until YOU light
        it up.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li><strong>Maximum control:</strong> You choose the speed, protocol, and equipment</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scalable:</strong> Upgrade speed by swapping optics, not the fiber (1G &#8594; 10G &#8594;
          100G &#8594; 400G)
        </li>
        <li><strong>Low latency:</strong> Direct physical path, nothing in between</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your responsibility:</strong> You maintain the electronics; provider maintains the physical
          fiber
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="result-grid mt-[10px]">
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Best For</div><div
            class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
          >
            Data center interconnect, high-bandwidth campus links, long-term needs
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Watch Out</div><div
            class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
          >
            Long lead times, fiber cuts need provider repair, contracts are 3&ndash;20 years
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="map-pin" /> P2P / Leased Lines</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> A <strong>dedicated, always-on, private circuit</strong> between
        exactly two locations. You get guaranteed bandwidth that nobody else shares. This is the oldest
        and most reliable form of WAN connectivity.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>
          <strong>Technologies:</strong> T1 (1.5 Mbps, legacy), T3 (45 Mbps, legacy), Ethernet Private
          Line (EPL), wavelength/DWDM
        </li>
        <li><strong>Symmetrical:</strong> Same speed in both directions</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Always on:</strong> No dialing, no tunnels &mdash; circuit is permanently up
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Layer 1 or 2:</strong> Provider gives you a transparent pipe; you run whatever protocol
          you want
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tip mt-[10px]">
        <Icon name="lightbulb" />
        <strong>Broadcast Use Case:</strong> P2P circuits are commonly used to carry audio/video feeds
        between studios and transmitter sites. A dedicated fiber P2P between your studio and tower ensures
        zero contention and lowest possible latency for on-air signals.
      </div>
    </div></TabPanel
  ><TabPanel id="wan-sdwan"
    ><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="brain" /> SD-WAN &mdash; Software-Defined WAN</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>What it is:</strong> A <strong>smart software layer</strong> that sits on top of your
        WAN links (broadband, DIA, MPLS, LTE) and <strong>automatically picks the best path</strong> for
        each type of traffic in real-time.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[8px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>
          <strong>How it works:</strong> SD-WAN appliances at each site monitor link quality (latency,
          jitter, loss) and dynamically steer traffic to the healthiest link
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Uses multiple links:</strong> Can combine broadband + DIA + LTE for redundancy and load
          balancing
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloud-friendly:</strong> Can send cloud traffic (Microsoft 365, Zoom) directly to the
          internet instead of backhauling through HQ
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Centralized management:</strong> Configure policies for all sites from one dashboard
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Built-in encryption:</strong> Creates IPsec tunnels between sites automatically
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="result-grid mt-[10px]">
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Replaces</div><div
            class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
          >
            MPLS for many use cases (cheaper, more flexible)
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Vendors</div><div
            class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
          >
            Cisco Viptela/Meraki, Fortinet, VMware VeloCloud, Palo Alto Prisma
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-item">
          <div class="ri-label">Still Needs</div><div
            class="ri-value font-sans text-[11.5px] text-text2"
          >
            Underlay circuits (broadband, DIA) &mdash; SD-WAN is the brain, not the road
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tip mt-[10px]">
        <Icon name="lightbulb" />
        <strong>Analogy:</strong> SD-WAN is like a GPS that picks the fastest route in real-time. MPLS
        is like a toll road &mdash; reliable and fast but expensive. Broadband is like a public highway
        &mdash; cheap but unpredictable. SD-WAN can use all of them and pick the best one for each trip.
      </div>
    </div></TabPanel
  ><TabPanel id="wan-vpn"
    ><div class="panel">
      <h3><Icon name="lock" /> VPN as a WAN Service</h3>
      <p>
        VPNs are often used as a <strong>low-cost alternative to dedicated WAN circuits</strong>.
        Instead of paying for MPLS or a leased line, you encrypt traffic over the internet.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-[14px]">Site-to-Site VPN</h3>
      <p>
        Connects two <strong>entire networks</strong> (office LANs) over the internet with a permanent
        encrypted tunnel. A VPN gateway/firewall at each site handles the encryption.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[4px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>Always-on tunnel between two firewalls/routers</li>
        <li>All devices on both LANs can communicate through the tunnel</li>
        <li>Uses IPsec (IKEv2) or GRE over IPsec</li>
        <li>No software needed on individual PCs &mdash; the router handles it</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="mt-[14px]">Remote Access VPN</h3>
      <p>
        Connects a <strong>single user</strong> (laptop/phone) to the corporate network from anywhere.
        The user runs VPN client software that creates a temporary tunnel.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-[4px] ml-[20px] list-disc leading-[1.9]">
        <li>User initiates connection; authenticates with credentials/MFA</li>
        <li>Common clients: Cisco AnyConnect, Palo Alto GlobalProtect, WireGuard, OpenVPN</li>
        <li>
          Full tunnel (all traffic through VPN) or split tunnel (only corporate traffic through VPN)
        </li>
        <li>Essential for work-from-home and traveling staff</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="warn mt-[10px]">
        <Icon name="triangle-exclamation" />️ <strong>VPN depends on internet quality:</strong> Unlike
        MPLS, VPNs ride on best-effort internet. If the internet link is congested or down, the VPN suffers.
        Use SD-WAN + VPN for automatic failover.
      </div>
    </div></TabPanel
  ></Tabs
>
